title: Panel types
tags: GUI
kind: overview
aliases: panel-type panels
summary: What a slot can show — a list, a detail, a file, the log… — the built-in panel types, your own, and what a panel keeps.

A ''panel type'' is a kind of content a slot can show. Each is a directory under
`~/.config/metafolder/gui/panel-types/` holding an `index.html` and its script; the
directory's name is the type's name. The built-in ones are installed there with the
configuration, so they can be read and changed like the rest of it
([[Updating the configuration]]); a new directory there is a new panel type,
listed next to the others ([[Writing a panel type]]).

Choose one with the panel menu of the slot's header, with `panel:set type <name>`,
or with the `t` keys: <<key "panel:set type metarecord-list" "t l">> for the list,
<<key "panel:set type metarecord-detail" "t d">> the detail,
<<key "panel:set type file" "t f">> the file, <<key "panel:set type file-manager" "t m">>
the file manager…

|!Panel type |!Shows |
|[[metarecord-list panel]] |the metarecords a query selects, as a table or a grid; the main source of the selection |
|[[metarecord-detail panel]] |every field of the selected metarecord |
|`file` |a preview of the selected file — image, video, audio, text, PDF — or a folder's thumbnails |
|`file-manager` |the file tree on disk, tracked or not, with file operations |
|[[treeref panel]] |a tree of metarecords (a `tree_ref` field such as `mfr_path` or tags), from the roots down |
|[[recent panel]] |the metarecords most recently viewed in this GUI |
|[[repos panel]] |the repositories: load, create, unload, their running tasks |
|[[log panel]] |the event log, revision by revision |
|[[log-detail panel]] |what the revision selected in the log changed, field by field |
|[[trash panel]] |the repository's trash |
|[[duplicates panel]] |groups of byte-identical files |
|`sync` |synchronisation between two repositories |
|[[message panel]] |the workspace's messages: status messages, commands run, shell output, the daemon's reports |
|[[workspace-info panel]] |the workspace's variables, live — for debugging panels and scripts |
|[[help panel]] |this documentation |
|[[hello panel]] |a minimal example to start a panel type from |

The [[Panel]] catalog has a note for each.

!! What a panel keeps

A panel keeps its whole state for the session, per workspace, even while another
panel type is displayed in its slot: switching back finds it as it was.

Being ''built'' is distinct from being ''shown''. When the GUI starts, it builds every
installed panel type once, hidden, in the first workspace, so that every panel's
commands exist from the start; that costs the panels' construction only — a panel
defers its expensive work (a directory listing, the log, streaming a file) until it is
first displayed, and a hidden panel asked to refresh several times refreshes once when
it is shown. At the same moment, the GUI reports in the message log every key bound
to a command that no panel and no builtin defines ([[Commands]]).

